:= cmd.Context()\n            skills := skillsFlag\n            if len(skills) == 0 {\n                // Fall back to configured skills for this agent.\n                s, err := settings.Load(ctx)\n                if err != nil { return err }\n                if s != nil { skills = s.ReviewSkillsFor(types.AgentName(agentFlag)) }\n                if len(skills) == 0 {\n                    return errors.New(\"no --skills provided and no configured skills for agent; run 'entire review --edit'\")\n                }\n            }\n            return runAttach(ctx, cmd.OutOrStdout(), args[0], types.AgentName(agentFlag), attachOptions{\n                force:        force,\n                reviewSkills: skills,\n            })\n        },\n    }\n    cmd.Flags().BoolVarP(&force, \"force\", \"f\", false, \"skip confirmation and amend the last commit\")\n    cmd.Flags().StringVarP(&agentFlag, \"agent\", \"a\", string(agent.DefaultAgentName), \"agent that created the session\")\n    cmd.Flags().StringSliceVar(&skillsFlag, \"skills\", nil, \"review skills that were run (default: configured skills for agent)\")\n    return cmd\n}\n```\n\n## Wiring\n\n`newReviewCmd()` already has the cobra.Command; just append:\n\n```go\ncmd.AddCommand(newReviewAttachCmd())\n```\n\nCobra is happy with a parent that has both `RunE` and subcommands — `entire review` runs the default spawn flow, `entire review attach <id>` runs the subcommand.\n\n## Tradeoffs vs. `entire attach --review`\n\n- **`entire review attach`**: the feature stays grouped under `review`. Users reading `entire review --help` see all review-related operations in one place. The attach code is shared via a thin wrapper.\n- **`entire attach --review`**: one less command surface, but the flag composes with other attach flags (`--force`, `--agent`) which is slightly confusing and splits \"review\" discovery across two commands.\n\nI'd pick your option — `entire review attach`. Discoverability wins, and the implementation cost is