{"parentUuid":"1b58822a-679c-4b12-99f5-f18a254089ca","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013S2SnT3cg7mUUnypmqyx1c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Committed as `cd5c391b4`.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe existing-checkpoint guard at `attach.go:163` checked `existingState.LastCheckpointID` — the session state file's record of which checkpoint it belongs to. But that only protects sessions whose state file tracks their checkpoint. A session already recorded in the HEAD checkpoint with no `LastCheckpointID` in state (file deleted, never written, or a condensation path that didn't update it) bypassed the guard.\n\nOnce past the guard:\n1. `resolveCheckpointID(headCommit)` read HEAD's trailer and returned the existing checkpoint ID.\n2. `WriteCommitted` → `findSessionIndex` at `committed.go:570` dedups by `SessionID` — same `SessionID` returns the same index.\n3. The write then **overwrote** the existing session's `metadata.json`, transcript, prompt, etc. with review-flavored data.\n\n## Fix\n\nDefense-in-depth: when review-attaching to an existing checkpoint, read the checkpoint's sessions via `store.ReadSessionContentByID(ctx, checkpointID, sessionID)` and refuse if found. Same error shape as the existing guard so users see a consistent message across both detection paths.\n\nRegression test `TestAttach_ReviewWithExistingCheckpointErrorsEvenWithoutSessionState` verifies it: first attach writes state + checkpoint, then deletes the state file to simulate the gap, then the review-attach must error instead of overwriting. Verified by stashing the fix — test fails without it, passes with it.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: this only checks against the v1 store (`cpkg.NewGitStore`). Users on v2-only mode could still hit the same overwrite in their v2 tree. Straightforward follow-up if relevant — a parallel `ReadSessionContentByID` check against the v2 store when `IsCheckpointsV2Enabled`."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn",